title ALES179 — Lotus's stereo_chorus: the triangle-LFO modulated delay mixed DRY-PLUS-WET carried into stereo, the SAME LFO and mix on BOTH channels, each channel read off ITS OWN frozen snapshot — the fourth rung of the stereo MODULATION class, the vibrato's parent
prompt Autonomous loop lap after ALES178: recur through agent-doable work Lindy-first crux-first, self-approve the design round, send each finished increment to origin and xykj61 as its own signed round, STOP at custody gates.
think ALES178 carried the vibrato (the modulated delay heard WET-ONLY) into stereo. The vibrato is exactly ALES71's chorus with the dry removed and the wet at full: chorus[i] = saturate(dry[i] + vibrato[i]). Crux-first: the natural parent rung is the chorus itself carried into stereo — the same shared LFO on both channels, each on its own snapshot, but now the delayed voice mixed UNDER THE DRY at a named level. That mix returns the one fault the vibrato dropped, BadLevel, so StereoChorusError carries five faults where the vibrato carried four. Simpler than the flanger (which adds a per-channel feedback path reading the live buffer), so chorus is the crux before flanger.
think chorus's five checks were inline, so factored them into a shared pub precheck(clip_len, start, count, centre, depth, period, num, den) mirroring ALES72's vibrato.precheck and ALES75's ring_mod.precheck, and had mono chorus call it; ales_chorus_witness re-ran GREEN (additive). Composed stereo_chorus mirroring stereo_vibrato: StereoChorusError = chorus.ChorusError reused whole, validate ONCE against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted, then run chorus.chorus per channel with the SAME LFO and mix. Each channel reads its OWN frozen snapshot — no cross-channel snapshot.
think Proved five laws: stereo chorus law (each channel == mono chorus byte for byte, channels genuinely differing); vibrato-family law (a full-wet stereo chorus == the dry master plus ALES178's stereo_vibrato PER CHANNEL sample for sample — imported stereo_vibrato and proved stereo_chorus[i] = saturate(dry[i] + stereo_vibrato[i]) on both); dry-identity law (num=0 the exact dry master on both); image law (identical-channel master stays identical AND a right-is-half-left pan kept OFF the rail keeps its L:R ratio, the read and mix linear in the input away from saturation); balance/silence/atomicity/degenerate (five refusals — BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange — each leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count 0 the identity).
obs No custody gate reached — pure local i16 DSP siloed to lotus/, a self-approved design round. Built and ran on metal; witness GREEN through Rishi, TAME style bans clean (opening triad, ≥2 asserts/fn with // invariant, no @memcpy, named errors with try, triangle+interpolation+mix in i64, dry-plus-wet sum saturating once), scan roster clean. The stereo modulation class now holds the amplitude pair (tremolo, ring modulator) AND the delay-line pair vibrato/chorus (wet-only child, dry-plus-wet parent). The next Lindy rung is the stereo flanger (a per-channel feedback path reading the live buffer — the harder crux, the first stereo modulation that reads its own output), or auto_pan.
